{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":15946829,"ns":0,"title":"Priscilla K. Coleman","revisions":[{"revid":1372282627,"parentid":1356835546,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:20:10Z","size":24124,"sha1":"6caa6bb0376887702f300c8ad86dcd51a133d9cc","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|American  psychologist and researcher}}\n{{For|the court room sketch artist|Priscilla Coleman (artist)}}\n{{Use mdy dates|date=April 2012}}\n{{Infobox scientist\n| name                      = Priscilla Kari Coleman\n| native_name_lang          =\n| image                     = <!--(as myimage.jpg, no 'File:')-->\n| image_size                = \n| alt                       = \n| caption                   = \n| birth_date                = <!-- {{Birth date|YYYY|MM|DD}} -->\n| birth_place               = \n| death_date                = <!-- {{Death date and age|YYYY|MM|DD|YYYY|MM|DD}} \n                           (death date then birth date) -->\n| death_place               = \n| resting_place             = \n| resting_place_coordinates = <!-- \n                      {{Coord|LAT|LONG|type:landmark|display=inline,title}} -->\n| other_names               = \n| citizenship               = \n| nationality               = \n| fields                    = [[Developmental psychology]]\n| workplaces                = [[Bowling Green State University]]\n| patrons                   = \n| education                 = [[Southern Connecticut State University]], [[James Madison University]], [[West Virginia University]]\n| thesis_title              = Maternal self-efficacy beliefs as predictors of parenting competence and toddlers' emotional, social, and cognitive development\n| thesis_url                = https://www.worldcat.org/oclc/40517559\n| thesis_year               = 1998\n| doctoral_advisor          = Katherine H. Karraker\n| academic_advisors         = \n| doctoral_students         = \n| notable_students          = \n| known_for                 = Research regarding the proposed link between [[abortion and mental health|abortion and mental health problems]]\n| author_abbrev_bot         = \n| author_abbrev_zoo         = \n| influences                = \n| influenced                = \n| awards                    = \n| signature                 = <!--(filename only)-->\n| signature_alt             = \n| website                   = <!-- {{URL|www.example.com}} -->\n| footnotes                 = \n| spouse                    = \n| children                  =  \n}}\n'''Priscilla K. Coleman''' is a retired<ref name=\"Weber-2023\">{{cite news |last1=Weber |first1=Lauren |last2=McGinley |first2=Laurie |last3=Ovalle |first3=David |last4=Sellars |first4=Frances Stead |name-list-style=amp |date=April 13, 2023 |title=Unpacking the flawed science cited in the Texas abortion pill ruling |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2023/04/13/abortion-pill-safety/ |newspaper=Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286 |access-date=April 20, 2023}}</ref> Professor of Human Development and Family Studies in the School of Family and Consumer Sciences at [[Bowling Green State University]], Ohio.<ref name=\"BGSU\">{{cite web |title=Priscilla Coleman, Ph.D (2002) |url=http://www.bgsu.edu/colleges/edhd/directory/fcs/page57559.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100603012935/http://www.bgsu.edu/colleges/edhd/directory/fcs/page57559.html |archive-date=June 3, 2010 |website=[[Bowling Green State University|BGSU]] |location=[[Bowling Green, Ohio|Bowling Green, OH]] |access-date=July 22, 2023}}</ref> She is the author (or co-author) of a number of disputed academic papers, which claim to have found a [[statistical correlation]] or [[causality|causal relationship]] between [[Abortion and mental health|abortion and mental health problems]].<ref name=\"pas\">{{cite web |last=Bazelon |first=Emily |author-link=Emily Bazelon |date=January 21, 2007 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2007/01/21/magazine/21abortion.t.html?_r=1&pagewanted=all |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110323100736/https://www.nytimes.com/2007/01/21/magazine/21abortion.t.html?_r=1&pagewanted=all |archive-date=March 23, 2011 |title=Is there a Post-Abortion Syndrome? |website=The New York Times}}</ref><ref name=\"now\"/><ref name=Gutt_2012-03-05 >{{ cite web | url=https://www.guttmacher.org/news-release/2012/study-purporting-show-link-between-abortion-and-mental-health-outcomes-decisively | title=Study Purporting to Show Link Between Abortion and Mental Health Outcomes Decisively Debunked - Journal's Editor Agrees that Priscilla Coleman's Conclusions Are Not Supported By Her Analyses | last= | first= | work=[[Guttmacher Institute]] | date=2012-03-05 | quote=A study purporting to show a causal link between abortion and subsequent mental health problems has fundamental analytical errors that render its conclusions invalid, according to researchers at the University of California, San Francisco (UCSF) and the Guttmacher Institute. This conclusion has been confirmed by the editor of the journal in which the study appeared. Most egregiously, the study, by Priscilla Coleman and colleagues, did not distinguish between mental health outcomes that occurred before abortions and those that occurred afterward, but still claimed to show a causal link between abortion and mental disorders.}}</ref>\n\nHer research has mostly met with a poor reception from her professional colleagues, and at least one of her manuscripts (originally published in ''Frontiers in Psychology'') was retracted from the scientific literature due to not meeting the standards of the journal.<ref>{{Cite journal |date=2022 |title=Retraction: The turnaway study: A case of self-correction in science upended by political motivation and unvetted findings |journal=Frontiers in Psychology |volume=13 |doi=10.3389/fpsyg.2022.1130026 |pmid=36636664 |issn=1664-1078|doi-access=free |author1=Frontiers Editorial Office |pmc=9831048 }}</ref> In a separate case, researchers were unable to [[reproducibility|reproduce]] Coleman's results on abortion and mental health despite using the same dataset,<ref name=\"steinberg\"/> and have described her findings as \"logically inconsistent\" and potentially \"substantially inflated\" by faulty methodology.<ref name=\"wa-po\"/> The [[American Psychological Association]] (APA) and other major medical bodies have concluded that the evidence does not support a link between abortion and mental health problems,<ref name=\"apa-review\">{{cite web | publisher = [[American Psychological Association]] | url = http://www.apa.org/pi/women/programs/abortion/index.aspx | title = Mental Health and Abortion | access-date =February 3, 2011 | date = August 2008}}</ref> and an APA panel charged with reviewing the evidence were similarly critical of the methodology of Coleman's studies.<ref name=\"now\"/> Coleman has responded that she is not the only qualified scientist whose research suggests that abortion may have serious mental health risks for many women.<ref name=\"wa-po\"/>\n\n==Biography and publications==\nColeman graduated from [[Southern Connecticut State University]] in 1988 with a [[Bachelor's degree|B.A.]] in Psychology. She obtained a [[Master's degree|M.A.]] in General Psychology from [[James Madison University]] in 1992, and a [[Doctorate|Ph.D.]] in Life-Span Developmental Psychology from [[West Virginia University]] in 1998.<ref name=\"BGSU\"/><ref>[http://www.bgsu.edu/downloads/cas/file36091.pdf CV] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121002124238/http://www.bgsu.edu/downloads/cas/file36091.pdf |date=October 2, 2012 }}</ref> She is the founder and director of the [[World Expert Consortium for Abortion Research and Education]] (WECARE),<ref name=\"WE ARE\">{{cite web |title=Priscilla K. Coleman, Ph.D., Director |url=http://wecareexperts.org/content/priscilla-k-coleman-phd-director |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230722212813/https://www.wecareexperts.org/content/priscilla-k-coleman-phd-director/ |archive-date=July 22, 2023 |website=WECARE |access-date=July 22, 2023}}</ref> which has been described by a [[Pro-choice and pro-life|pro-choice]] educational foundation as an \"anti-choice misinformation group\".<ref name=\"NARAL\">{{cite web |title=Independent Medical Experts |url=https://tellthewholestorynow.com/independent-medical-experts/ |website=NARAL Pro-Choice America Foundation |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230603154607/https://tellthewholestorynow.com/independent-medical-experts/ |archive-date=June 3, 2023 |access-date=July 22, 2023}}</ref>\n\nColeman's three most cited papers,<ref>{{cite web |title=Priscilla K. Coleman |url=https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=S--K9lkAAAAJ&hl=en&oi=sra |website=[[Google Scholar]] |access-date=July 28, 2023}}</ref> co-authored with Katherine Hildebrandt Karraker, concern parental [[self-efficacy]] and its consequences.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Coleman |first1=Priscilla K. |last2=Karraker |first2=Katherine H. |name-list-style=amp |date=1998 |title=Self-Efficacy and Parenting Quality: Findings and Future Applications |journal=[[Developmental Review]] |volume=18 |issue=1 |pages=47–85 |publisher=[[Academic Press]] |doi=10.1006/drev.1997.0448 |issn=0273-2297}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Coleman |first1=Priscilla K. |last2=Karraker |first2=Katherine Hildebrandt |name-list-style=amp |date=2000 |title=Parenting Self-Efficacy Among Mothers of School-Age Children: Conceptualization, Measurement, and Correlates |journal=Family Relations |volume=49 |issue=1 |pages=13–24 |publisher=[[National Council on Family Relations]] |doi=10.1111/j.1741-3729.2000.00013.x |jstor=585698 |issn=0197-6664}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Coleman |first1=Priscilla K. |last2=Karraker |first2=Katherine Hildebrandt |name-list-style=amp |date=2003 |title=Maternal self-efficacy beliefs, competence in parenting, and toddlers' behavior and developmental status |journal=[[Infant Mental Health Journal]] |volume=24 |issue=2 |pages=126–148 |publisher=[[Wiley (publisher)|Wiley]]|doi=10.1002/imhj.10048 |issn=1097-0355}}</ref> She has also published a series of articles reporting a [[statistical correlation|correlation]] between [[abortion and mental health|induced abortion and mental-health problems]], findings which have proven controversial.\n\nIn 2011, Coleman published a [[meta-analysis]] of 22 studies, half of which were her own, in the ''[[British Journal of Psychiatry]]''. In this paper, she claimed to have found that women who had undergone an abortion \"experienced an 81% increased risk of mental health problems, and nearly 10% of the incidence of mental health problems was shown to be directly attributable to abortion\".<ref name=\"Coleman\">{{cite journal |last=Coleman |first=Priscilla K. |date=2011 |title=Abortion and mental health: quantitative synthesis and analysis of research published 1995–2009\n|journal=The British Journal of Psychiatry |volume=199 |issue=3 |pages=180–186 |pmid=21881096 |doi=10.1192/bjp.bp.110.077230 |doi-access=free}}</ref> The [[Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists]] noted that Coleman's results conflict with those of four previous literature reviews, all of which found that women who have abortions did not face an increased risk of mental health problems. The College suggested that Coleman's results were due to her failure to control for pre-existing mental-health problems, which tend to be more prevalent in women having abortions.<ref>{{Cite web |publisher=Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists |url=http://www.rcog.org.uk/what-we-do/campaigning-and-opinions/statement/rcog-statement-bjpsych-paper-mental-health-risks-and-a |title=RCOG statement on BJPsych paper on mental health risks and abortion |date=September 1, 2011 |access-date=September 5, 2011| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111117020102/https://www.rcog.org.uk/what-we-do/campaigning-and-opinions/statement/rcog-statement-bjpsych-paper-mental-health-risks-and-a| archive-date=17 November 2011}}</ref> This meta-analysis was also criticized by Julia Steinberg and a number of other researchers, who wrote in 2012 that it contained seven significant errors, as well as three shortcomings of the included studies. Steinberg et al. concluded that these errors and shortcomings \"render the meta-analysis' conclusions invalid.\"<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Steinberg |first1=Julia R. |last2=Trussell |first2=James |last3=Hall |first3=Kelli |last4=Guthrie |first4=Kate |name-list-style=amp |date=2012 |title=Fatal flaws in a recent meta-analysis on abortion and mental health |url=https://www.contraceptionjournal.org/article/S0010-7824(12)00150-3/fulltext |url-access=subscription |journal=Contraception |volume=86 |issue=5 |pages=430–437 |doi=10.1016/j.contraception.2012.03.012 |pmid=22579105 |pmc=3646711}}</ref>\n\n=== Reception and reaction ===\nThe statistical methods Coleman and her co-authors use have been criticized by the [[American Psychological Association]] (APA).<ref name=\"now\">{{cite news | url = https://www.pbs.org/now/transcript/329.html | archive-url = https://archive.today/20130415143550/http://www.pbs.org/now/transcript/329.html | url-status = dead | archive-date = April 15, 2013 | title = NOW with David Brancaccio | publisher = [[PBS]] |quote =HINOJOSA: In emails, two prominent independent scientists, on a panel that is reviewing the scientific literature for the American Psychological Association told us the studies have 'inadequate or inappropriate' controls and don't adequately control 'for women's mental health prior to the pregnancy and abortion.'}}</ref> An APA panel found that studies by Coleman and her co-authors have \"inadequate or inappropriate\" controls and don't adequately consider \"women's mental health prior to the pregnancy and abortion.\"<ref name=\"now\"/> Coleman defended her methodology, arguing that it is consistent with recommendations in the Handbook of Research Synthesis and Meta-Analysis.<ref>{{cite news |work=BJP |title=Re: Abortion and Mental Health |author=Priscilla K. Coleman |url=http://bjp.rcpsych.org/content/199/3/180/reply#bjrcpsych_el_34220}}</ref>\n\nColeman and her coauthors have also been criticized by other researchers in the field over the meaning and [[reproducibility]] of their data. Psychologist [[Brenda Major]] criticized one of Coleman's studies, saying that it did not distinguish [[correlation does not imply causation|correlation and cause]]; that the direction of causality could be reversed, with psychiatric problems leading to a greater incidence of abortion; and that the study failed to control for factors such as relationship stability and education.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.toledoblade.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20040122/NEWS09/101220084 | title=After decades of research, evaluating abortion's effect still difficult | work=Toledo Blade | date=January 22, 2004 | last=Laidman | first=Jenni | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060110201926/http://www.toledoblade.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=%2F20040122%2FNEWS09%2F101220084 | archive-date=January 10, 2006 | df=mdy-all }}</ref> Jillian Henderson, a professor of gynecology, and Katharine Miller wrote to the ''[[Journal of Anxiety Disorders]]'', saying, \"We believe that Cougle, et al., operate with strong political views regarding abortion, and unfortunately their biases appear to have resulted in serious methodological flaws in the analysis published in your journal. <nowiki>[Reardon, Coleman and Cougle]</nowiki> are involved in building a literature to be used in efforts to restrict access to abortion.\"<ref>{{cite web |first=James C. |last=Coyne |author-link=James C. Coyne |date=November 15, 2011 |title=More on Review Claiming Abortion Hurts Women's Mental Health |url=http://www.psychologytoday.com/blog/the-skeptical-sleuth/201111/more-review-claiming-abortion-hurts-womens-mental-health |website=[[Psychology Today]] |location=New York |publisher=Sussex Publishers}}</ref> [[Nancy Russo]], a psychology professor and abortion researcher, examined two of Coleman's articles and found that when the methodological flaws in the studies were corrected, the supposed correlation between abortion and poor mental health disappeared.<ref name=\"pas\"/>\n\nOther researchers were unable to reproduce Coleman's analysis of the [[National Comorbidity Survey]], which she had used to support an association between abortion and depression or substance abuse.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Coleman |first1=Priscilla K. |last2=Coyle |first2=Catherine T. |last3=Shuping |first3=Martha |last4=Rue |first4=Vincent M. |author-link4=Vincent Rue |name-list-style=amp |date=2009 |title=Induced abortion and anxiety, mood, and substance abuse disorders: isolating the effects of abortion in the national comorbidity survey |journal=Journal of Psychiatric Research |volume=43 |issue=8 |pages=770–6 |pmid=19046750 |doi=10.1016/j.jpsychires.2008.10.009}}</ref> Coleman and her colleagues, according to one review, failed to control for pre-existing mental health problems and for other risk factors for mental health problems, such as sexual or physical violence.<ref name=\"steinberg\">{{cite journal |last1=Steinberg |first1=Julia R. |last2=Finer |first2=Lawrence B. |name-list-style=amp |date=2011 |title=Examining the association of abortion history and current mental health: A reanalysis of the National Comorbidity Survey using a common-risk-factors model |journal=Social Science & Medicine |volume=72 |issue=1 |pages=72–82 |pmid=21122964 |doi=10.1016/j.socscimed.2010.10.006}}</ref> Julia Steinberg, one of the researchers attempting to verify Coleman's findings, said: \"We were unable to reproduce the most basic tabulations of Coleman and colleagues... Moreover, their findings were logically inconsistent with other published research—for example, they found higher rates of depression in the last month than other studies found during respondents' entire lifetimes. This suggests that the results were substantially inflated.\"<ref name=\"wa-po\"/>\n\nColeman initially responded that her analysis had used different methods and examined long-term psychological problems.<ref name=\"wa-po\">{{cite news |last=Stein |first=Rob |date=December 13, 2010 |title=Study disputes link between abortion and mental health problems |url=http://voices.washingtonpost.com/checkup/2010/12/study_disputes_abortion_mental.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101215165940/http://voices.washingtonpost.com/checkup/2010/12/study_disputes_abortion_mental.html |archive-date=December 15, 2010 |newspaper=Washington Post}}</ref> Subsequently, she and her coauthors issued a correction to their paper, stating that they had made an error in weighting the study variables. After correcting their error, the association between abortion and some mental-health problems weakened or disappeared, but the authors concluded that \"fortunately, the overall pattern of the results has not changed very much\".<ref name=\"correction\">{{cite journal |last1=Coleman |first1=Priscilla K. |last2=Coyle |first2=Catherine T. |last3=Shuping |first3=Martha |last4=Rue |first4=Vincent M. |name-list-style=amp |date=2011 |journal=Journal of Psychiatric Research |title=Corrigendum to \"Induced abortion and anxiety, mood, and substance abuse disorders: Isolating the effects of abortion in the national comorbidity survey\" [Journal of Psychiatric Research 2009;43:770–776] |url= |volume=45 |issue=8 |pages=1133–1134 |doi=10.1016/j.jpsychires.2011.06.010 |doi-access=}}</ref> Separately, the journal editor and the [[principal investigator]] of the NCS (from which Coleman had drawn her data) opined that, in light of the concerns raised, Coleman et al.'s analysis \"does not support their assertions that abortions led to [[psychopathology]].\"<ref name=\"kessler\">{{cite journal |last1=Kessler |first1=Ronald C. |last2=Schatzberg |first2=Alan F. |author-link2=Alan Schatzberg |name-list-style=amp |date=2012 |title=Commentary on Abortion Studies of Steinberg and Finer (Social Science & Medicine 2011; 72:72–82) and Coleman (Journal of Psychiatric Research 2009;43:770–6 & Journal of Psychiatric Research 2011;45:1133–4) |journal=Journal of Psychiatric Research |volume=46 |issue=3 |pages=410–411 |doi=10.1016/j.jpsychires.2012.01.021}}</ref> Despite the correction, further concerns about the accuracy of Coleman's analysis were raised;<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Steinberg |first1=Julia R. |last2=Finer |first2=Lawrence B. |name-list-style=amp |date=2012 |title=Coleman, Coyle, Shuping, and Rue make false statements and draw erroneous conclusions in analyses of abortion and mental health using the National Comorbidity Survey |journal=Journal of Psychiatric Research |volume=46 |issue=3 |pages=407–408 |doi=10.1016/j.jpsychires.2012.01.019 |pmid=22348853 |pmc=3699180}}</ref> Coleman responded to these criticisms and pointed to other work she had published.<ref name=\"coleman-response\">{{cite journal |last=Coleman |first=Priscilla K.|date=2012 |title=Response to Dr Steinberg and Dr Finer's letter to the Editor |journal=Journal of Psychiatric Research |volume=46 |issue=3 |pages=408&ndash;9 |doi=10.1016/j.jpsychires.2012.01.020}}</ref>\n\n==Court witness==\nIn an August 2022 [[Michigan]] court case challenging a 1931 abortion ban, Coleman was hired by abortion opponents to provide testimony.<ref name=\"Fox2Detroit\"/> The judge in the case, Jacob James Cunningham, stated: \"Dr Coleman's testimony is dismissed as not credible, in a practical sense, completely called into question during cross-examination, nor helpful in assisting the court in defeating the [[plaintiff]]'s request for a preliminary injunction. The court affords testimony no weight,\" and also stated: \"Specifically, testimony established that 22 studies were aggregated in the metadata study. Of those 22, 11 were authored by the witness and only 14 data sets were used. Presumably, the testimony revealed 11 of them created by the witness herself.\"<ref name=\"Fox2Detroit\">{{cite web |date=August 19, 2022 |title=Oakland County judge rips pro-life witnesses, says both are not credible |url=https://www.fox2detroit.com/news/oakland-county-judge-rips-pro-life-witnesses-says-both-are-not-credible |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220820215432/https://www.fox2detroit.com/news/oakland-county-judge-rips-pro-life-witnesses-says-both-are-not-credible |archive-date=August 20, 2022 |website=FOX 2 Detroit |quote=Dr. Coleman – deemed an expert in psychology of abortion, decision-making and mental health outcomes – testified about her research and publications regarding mental health impact to women who have abortions. \"On cross-examination, in the court's eyes, Dr. Coleman's credibility was seriously called into question and her statistics and conclusions of her testimony revealed unhelpful and biased information for the court's instant inquiry,\" Cunningham said. \"Notable was Dr. Coleman's indication that although there were 'very few' psychological studies and instances of rape or incest, resulting in birth, she nevertheless concluded - stunningly - that only 20% of those mothers harbor resentment towards that child.\" Cunningham said she alluded to bias and pro-choice interest groups and provided analysis of her own data. \"Specifically, testimony established that 22 studies were aggregated in the metadata study. Of those 22, 11 were authored by the witness and only 14 data sets were used. Presumably, the testimony revealed 11 of them created by the witness herself,\" he said. When the plaintiff got a chance to question her data, Cunningham said her research didn't hold up.}}</ref>\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist|2}}\n\n==External links==\n*[https://www.bgsu.edu/education-and-human-development/family-and-consumer-sciences/faculty-staff/priscilla-coleman.html BGSU faculty page]\n*{{Google Scholar id|S--K9lkAAAAJ}}\n*[http://www.labome.org/expert/usa/bowling/coleman/p-k-coleman-529764.html List of publications by Coleman]\n\n{{Authority control}}\n\n{{DEFAULTSORT:Coleman, Priscilla}}\n[[Category:Living people]]\n[[Category:Bowling Green State University faculty]]\n[[Category:James Madison University alumni]]\n[[Category:Southern Connecticut State University alumni]]\n[[Category:West Virginia University alumni]]\n[[Category:American anti-abortion activists]]\n[[Category:American developmental psychologists]]\n[[Category:Year of birth missing (living people)]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-17T19:38:17Z","lastrevid":1372282627,"length":24124}]}}